question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=0.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) \\\\ f(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{0+1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{(0+1)^{2}}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(0+1)^{3}}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & =(0)+(1)(x-0)+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-0)^{2}+\\frac{(2)}{3!}(x-0)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=0.5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1.5 & ≈(0.5)-\\frac{1}{2}(0.5)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(0.5)^{3} \\\\  & =0.417\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126875/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126875"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection/d5d616dfff9b356a048a3bc19019f0875e57850733ce63894f310b3733271ceb" :answer/id #uuid "f402e085-7c9e-5902-84fa-aa694e9b204f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.746" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection/f9378424d1fb8ed709cf83b2f9b03ec28d57d5c704fc1aaeee2e8628ffdbdbdb" :answer/id #uuid "6ffed095-ed97-5bd4-9101-ccf3484fc367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.732" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection/29427bd1b56828b1d2904e53f717ae4db1f18cf690df26c8431b1cc7d8f9adf0" :answer/id #uuid "63eb126b-998b-5738-99d3-79c8d12d0f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.739" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection/79a854fe7b3a86e2a5b3ba9ee6e3053f52b2d873a59eca3034b90616d4d0ea97" :answer/id #uuid "6e983fbc-7a3b-5d32-ba55-edc40db4982c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.735" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection/a96814ed9c4a3fdd46acee1a8c3bf14a7a41aaa782c8803c793ac012efd03aac" :answer/id #uuid "857b6e71-5737-5493-8f07-a81600361b2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈0.744" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3b40202-1b04-576e-97dc-7280df173f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126877/selection/79a854fe7b3a86e2a5b3ba9ee6e3053f52b2d873a59eca3034b90616d4d0ea97" :answer-field/choices ["q-126877/selection/d5d616dfff9b356a048a3bc19019f0875e57850733ce63894f310b3733271ceb" "q-126877/selection/f9378424d1fb8ed709cf83b2f9b03ec28d57d5c704fc1aaeee2e8628ffdbdbdb" "q-126877/selection/29427bd1b56828b1d2904e53f717ae4db1f18cf690df26c8431b1cc7d8f9adf0" "q-126877/selection/79a854fe7b3a86e2a5b3ba9ee6e3053f52b2d873a59eca3034b90616d4d0ea97" "q-126877/selection/a96814ed9c4a3fdd46acee1a8c3bf14a7a41aaa782c8803c793ac012efd03aac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126877" :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126877/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-9608"] :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{10}.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(10-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{216} \\\\  & =3.162\\,04,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
